\version "2.24.0"
% c182 (A&S Unit 5, PDF 76, 83): 239 Hn 3-4 unis. C4 (were E4/E3: parallel fifths D4-E4 with the cellos' G2-A2 and G3-E3 with the basses' C2-A1); 242 harp RH F4 (was D4) and Fl 2 F5 -> E5 at 242-243 (Fl 2 rested 235-246), so the ii7 has its third, above the seventh C5 of Vln II (F under C would make fifths F-C -> E-B), falling to the root of III#.
% c129 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 102): 238 Hn 3 D4, Hn 4 G3 (were G3/F3, a second in the soft horn pedal); the seventh F stays in Cl 1 and violas at 238.3; Hn 3 A3-D4-E4 and Hn 4 F3-G3-E3 move by step or fourth.
% c122 (harmony; A&S Unit 21, PDF 367-368: a bass pedal persists audibly as the lowest voice through the chords above it; Unit 17, PDF 264: the S head's 235-238 expands the tonic over that pedal): 238 harp LH G2 (was G1), doubling the cellos' G2 at the unison, so the harp no longer puts the dominant root under the basses' C2 pedal for one bar; same string and pedal (G natural), right hand unchanged.
% MOVEMENT I'S SECOND THEME IN C MAJOR, bars 235-246 (cyclic-notes 'Owed' item 5): the violins' singing statement of the falling
% major head with its pickup (234) and the chromatic counter (clarinet 1 and violas), molto cantabile, over the C pedal (basses, timpani);
% movement I withheld it (its §2 item 1). 239-242: a continuation to A6, flutes and oboe with the violins.
% 243-246: movement I's horn call in major, E-C-B-C, in trumpets 1-2 with horns 1-2 two octaves below (cyclic-notes 'Owed' item 8);
% G pedal, then V4/3 (D bass), stringendo into the Più allegro.
% c020 (bars 235-246): 241-243 bass D-F-E-G: ii (241) - II6/5 (242) - III# (E major, 243 first half) - V (cadential 6/4, 243 b3 to 246) - I (247).
% The major-third cycle of movements I and II (C - A flat - F flat/E) closes inside C major: I (235) - III# - V (A&S PDF 395 points 5b and 6:
% III# in the arpeggiation I-III#-V, approached by II6 so that G does not move straight to G sharp; A&S PDF 456 Ex. 26-9 and PDF 463 point 7:
% I-tonicized III-V in major). The trumpets' and horns' E of the horn call is the root of the E-major chord; Vln I (lower divisi) has the only G sharp
% and takes it to G in the same voice (A&S PDF 394: no split cross relation, no doubled chromatic tone); the timpani (no E) wait for the G. Replaces c003's V6/5 of V at 242, which had the same aim (no I6 before the 6/4).

% c030 (cyclic lens; themes-mvt1.ly mvtOneThemeSCmajor, invariant 4 | 2.~ 8 8 | 4 2 4): 246 beat 4 Hn 2, Cl 1 and violas give the brass statement of
% movement I's second-theme head at 247 its pickup G3, so it opens with the rising sixth G-E of movement I bar 146 (Hn 1 + Vla at G3), as the violins' statement does at 234-235.
% c032 (harmony; A&S Unit 17, PDF 266-267, 276-277): 239-240 the Cl 1 + viola counter is movement I's own C-B-B flat | A (as at 235-236), a third under the tune (was A-G sharp-G | F: D-C over G-F, parallel 5ths with the tune); 240 Hn 3 F4 (was C4: E-C over the bass A-F, 5ths); 236 Bn 1 A3 (was F3: G-F under the tune's D-C, 5ths).
% c056 (playability; Adler part 1 PDF 30: without "div." a chord is played as a double stop): 243-245 violins marked div., unis. at the run in 245 (Vln II C4-E5 at 243 is a tenth across non-adjacent strings, impossible as a double stop).
% c118 (Adler ch. 2, PDF 39-40: at forte about six notes to a bow; a long slur under a crescendo must be broken into several bows): 246 the run's slur ends on F, the top note takes a new
% (down-) bow at the peak of the crescendo, as at 197-198 and 264 (c085); violas likewise, so the pickup G is up-bow and 247's ff downbeat a down-bow.
% c119 (Rimsky PDF 83: the bass of an inversion of the dominant chord is never doubled in an upper part): 245 Vln I lower note G4 (common tone from 244), not D4 over the V4/3's bass D
% c138 (expression; Casanelles ch. 4, PDF 151, 155; movement I v99 155-159 and v221 tTwo Hn 1, the same theme): the pickup G4 at 234 is slurred into 235 (rising sixth on one bow), with movement I's swells on the held E5 (235) and the tenuto A5 (236), a crescendo through 237 and a diminuendo on the C6 appoggiatura into B5 (238). Pitches and rhythm unchanged (themecheck).
% c238 (expression; Casanelles ch. 4, PDF 155: the lyrical legato swells into the note of arrival; PDF 149-150: a line held at one level sounds lifeless; ch. 6, PDF 290: a line is heard when the others are made quieter): 239-242 the S head's second phrase (Vln I-II, Fl 1, Ob 1) takes movement I's swells (recTwo: on the held note and the tenuto, then a diminuendo through the descent, as 235-238); at 243 strings and bassoons restart mf < under the horn call (Hn 1-2 ff, Tpt 1-2 f), so the E-major call is two levels above its accompaniment and the crescendo to ff at 247 is four bars long. Ob 1 re-takes f on the accented G sharp. Notes, slurs, the call and its dynamics unchanged.
% c310 (cyclic; A&S Unit 32, PDF 600-601 sec. 16: a chromatic area restates the piece's characteristic pitch relation, enharmonically reinterpreted (Appassionata E flat-F flat-E flat
%   becomes E flat-E natural-E flat); PDF 593 (Waldstein): III sharp gives up its chromatic tone to the diatonic one; PDF 588: a true enharmonic is heard, not only written):
%   243 Vln I upper desk G sharp5 -> G5 (was E5 held), so the E-major chord, the finale's only sound in the scherzo's key, puts the scherzo's last pitch (Vln I G sharp5, scherzo 369-376)
%   on top and lets it fall to G5, the same pitch and move as the finale's first bar (Vln I A flat5 -> G5, 1-2). One G sharp line, now in three octaves (Hn 4 G#3, Ob 1 + lower Vln I G#4, upper Vln I G#5);
%   the I-vi-IV-ii7-III#-V plan, the horn call and the 6/4 at 243.3 are unchanged; E5 stays in Tpt 1, Fl 2 and Vln II.
% c338 (expression; Casanelles ch. 4, PDF 151, 154-155: the transition between two notes of a line is where its expression lives, portamento being one of the transition techniques, and the lyrical legato slides into a swell on the note of arrival; Adler PDF 33: composers mark port. when they want a conscious slide):
%   236 Vln I and II mark the slide on the S head's rising sixth C5-A5, which the protected sul A already makes a shift on one string, into the tenuto A5 and its swell. Once only; 240 (ord., E string) has none. Pitches, slurs, hairpins unchanged; MIDI unchanged.
% c355 (Adler ch. 2, PDF 33: a glissando is a line between the noteheads 'with or without the word glissando (gliss.) above the line'): 236 'port.' above the slide C5-A5 in Vln I-II, under the 'sul A' line (was below the staff, beside the hairpin).
sHeadVlnOne = { \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.left.text = \markup \italic "sul A" e''2.~\startTextSpan\< e''8\> d''8) | c''4(\!-\tweak outside-staff-priority #340 ^\markup \italic "port." \glissando a''2--\< g''4\!) | f''4.(\< e''8 d''4 f''8 a''8) | c'''2(\> b''2)\!\stopTextSpan | e'''2.~(\f\<^\markup \italic "ord." e'''8\> d'''8) | c'''4(\! a'''2--\< g'''4\>) | f'''4.( e'''8 d'''4 c'''4) | d'''2( c'''2) | <gis' gis''>2\mf\<^\markup \italic "div." <g' g''>2 | <g' e''>4 <g' d''>2. | <g' b'>2 r8 \once \override TextScript.avoid-slur = #'outside g''8(^\markup \italic "unis." a'' b'' | \once \override Beam.positions = #'(-0.25 . 0.25) c'''8 d''' e''' f''') g'''2\! | }
sHeadVlnTwo = { \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.left.text = \markup \italic "sul A" e''2.~\startTextSpan\< e''8\> d''8) | c''4(\!-\tweak outside-staff-priority #340 ^\markup \italic "port." \glissando a''2--\< g''4\!) | f''4.(\< e''8 d''4 f''8 a''8) | c'''2(\> b''2)\!\stopTextSpan | e''2.~(\f\<^\markup \italic "ord." e''8\> d''8) | c''4(\! a''2--\< g''4\>) | f''4.( e''8 d''4 c''4) | d''2( c''2) | <e' b'>2\mf\<^\markup \italic "div." <c' e''>2 | <c' g'>4 <b g'>2. | <b f'>2 r8 \once \override TextScript.avoid-slur = #'outside g'8(^\markup \italic "unis." a' b' | c''8 d'' e'' f'') g''2\! | }
% c077: Cl 1's pickup at 246 is G4 (was G3), keeping the rising sixth into its S head at E5 (finale.ily 247)
% c216 (Adler PDF 188: let a doubled line carry while one player breathes): 242 Cl 1 breathes at its slur end (239-250 otherwise runs 12 bars, stringendo, into the ff S head); the violas hold the same notes.
% c250 (cyclic review; Rimsky-Korsakov, editor's preface PDF 18: 'the counter-melody on the violas and violoncellos doubled by the wood-wind will hardly be heard'
%   when the surrounding parts keep their marks; author's preface PDF 19-20: the orchestrator must 'detach a melody from its harmonic setting'):
%   239-242 the continuation of movement I's chromatic counter (Cl 1 + violas, C5-B4-B flat 4-A4 ... C4-D4) is f with the tune instead of mf under f harp, bassoons and low strings;
%   the harp's broken chords, which lie in the counter's own octave (A3-A4, D4-A4), drop to mf. Notes, swells and the c238 harp diminuendo at 242 unchanged.
% c278 (expression; Adler ch. 7, adler1 PDF 212 Ex. VII-63 (Mendelssohn, Hebrides: the second clarinet's independent line has its own swell, not the tune's),
%   PDF 188 Ex. VII-9 (Brahms 1/iv: the swell on the held note)): 236-237 the chromatic counter (Cl 1 + violas) swells on its one held note, the tied C5 of 236.3,
%   with the violins' crescendo on 236 and recedes on 237 while the violins climb to their C6 at 238 (the counter steps back as the tune peaks) instead of staying flat at p. Notes unchanged.
% c284 (orchestration; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. III, PDF 108: 'clarinets, on account of their tone quality, should rarely be set inside the horns, but in the upper register ... a chord of four horns may be completed by clarinets'; PDF 110-111: with trumpets in the chord the wood-wind forms the harmonic part above): 243-246 Cl 1 B4-C5 | G5 | F5 (was B3-C4 | G4 | F4, throat tones G4-F4 inside Hn 1-2's octave C4-C5 under the ff call), so the clarinet sits above the horns and the 7th F5 resolves to E5 at 247 in its own octave; pickup G4 at 246.4 kept (c077).
sHeadClOne = { r4 c''4(\p b'4 bes'4 | a'2) c''2~(\< | c''4\> bes'4 a'2\! | g'2 f'2) | r4 c''4(\f b'4 bes'4 | a'2) a'2~( | a'4 g'4 f'2 | c'2 d'2) \breathe | b'2\< c''2 | g''1 | f''1 | f''2. g'4\! | }
sHeadVla = { r4 c''4(\p b'4 bes'4 | a'2) c''2~(\< | c''4\> bes'4 a'2)\! | g'2( f'2) | r4 c''4(\f b'4 bes'4 | a'2) a'2~( | a'4 g'4 f'2) | c'2( d'2) | b2\< c'2 | g1 | f2 r8 g8( a b | c'8 d' e' f') g'4 g4\! | }
sHeadFluteOne = { R1*4 | e'''2.~(\f\< e'''8\> d'''8 | c'''4\! a'''2--\< g'''4\> | f'''4. e'''8 d'''4 c'''4 | d'''2 c'''2) | R1*2\! | r2 r8 g''8(\f a'' b'' | \once \override Beam.positions = #'(-0.25 . 0.25) c'''8 d''' e''' f''' g'''2) | }
% c083 (A&S Unit 15, PDF 233-234: III in major is identified only by its chromatic tones, "a complete chord would require a D sharp as well"; Rimsky-Korsakov tutti chords):
% 243 Ob 1 G sharp4 (was B4, the fifth, already in Hn 3, Bn 1, Cl 1, Vla, Vln II) at the unison with Vln I's lower divisi, then G4 with it: the third of III# was one half-section of violins
% against four brass on E; the unison keeps the chromatic tone in one voice (no octave doubling), and the 7th C of 242 still resolves to B in Vln II and Hn 3.
sHeadFluteTwo = { R1*7 | f''1\mf | e''2 r2 | R1*3 | }
sHeadOboeOne = { R1*4 | e''2.~(\f\< e''8\> d''8 | c''4\! a''2--\< g''4\> | f''4. e''8 d''4 c''4 | d''2 c''2) | gis'2->\f( g'2) | R1*3 | }
% c049 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 138-139: under a soft cantabile line, strings doubled by wood-wind and wood-wind doubled by horns are the combinations that cover it; reduce the
% sustaining parts): 235-238 Bn 1-2 rest (Bn 2 doubled the cellos, Bn 1 the horns' G-A-A-G); the harmony stays complete in Hn 3-4, cellos, basses and harp; they enter f at 239.
sHeadVc = { c1\p | c1 | d2 f,2 | g,1 | a,1\f | f,1 | d1 | d,1\> | e,2\mf\< g,2 | g,1 | d1 | d1\! | }
sHeadCb = { c,1\p~\cbaw | c,1~ | c,1~ | c,1 | a,,1\f | f,,1 | d,1 | d,1\> | e,2\mf\< g,2 | g,,1 | d,1 | d,1\! | }
sHeadBsnTwo = { R1*4 | a,1\f | f,1 | d1 | d,1\> | e,2\mf\< g,2 | g,1 | d1 | d1\! | }
% c082 (harmony; A&S Unit 27, PDF 473-476 and 479 point 11): from 245 beat 3 the horn call's C (Tpt 1-2, Hn 1-2) is an unresolved 4th over V4/3 that anticipates the tonic; the book's 4th replaces the 3rd.
% Bn 1 leaves B3 (a semitone under Hn 2's C4) for the 7th F3, which falls to E3 at 247 (after the head's G2 eighth): no B against C, and the 7th now has a voice that resolves it.
% c247 (texture; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. III, PDF 108: in a chain of chords the stationary parts go to the brass, the moving parts to the wood-wind;
%   two bassoons and two horns in four real parts, crossed, concords in the horns, the discord in the bassoon; PDF 112: correct progression of parts first):
%   239-242 Hn 3 C4-C4-D4-D4 and Hn 4 C4-A3-A3-A3 hold, Bn 1 moves E3-F3-F3 and takes the 7th C4 on 242.3 into B3 at 243 (was Bn 1 C4-C4-A3-D4 in unison with
%   the horns at 239 and 241, Hn 3 C4-F4-A3-D4-C4). 239 keeps c182's horn unison C4 (no fifths with the cellos' G2-A2); the chord's fifth E3 is now in Bn 1.
sHeadBsnOne = { R1*4 | e1\f | f1 | f1 | f2\> c'2 | b2\mf\< c'2 | c'4 b2. | b2 f2 | f1\! \breathe | }
sHeadHnThree = { g1\p | a1 | a1 | d'1 | c'1\mf | c'1 | d'1 | d'1 \breathe | b2\f r2 | R1*3 | }
% c082 (A&S Unit 27, PDF 465-466: a 7th must move to the next chord's tone of resolution; unresolved 7ths are rare): 239 Hn 4 E3 (was C4), so the 7th F of 238's V7 over the C pedal falls F3-E3 into vi; no other voice resolved it.
% c084 (orchestration; Casanelles PDF 277: sounds in the same range mask each other, fewer instruments in a range are clearer; PDF 289: forte brass is the densest, foregrounded layer; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 100: brass harmony in close order with no empty spaces; PDF 108: in forte the horns overwhelm the bassoons):
%   243-246 Hn 4 A3-G sharp3-G3-F3, then E3 at 247 (finale.ily). The brass's E-major chord at 243 was E3-B3-E4-E4-E5 with no third (the G sharp only in Ob 1 and half of Vln I); the V4/3's 7th at 245-246 was only in Bn 1 F3 under ff horns. Hn 3 f at 243 to match Hn 4 on the shared staff.
% c226 (playability; Adler ch. 9, adler1 PDF 292 and ch. 10, PDF 309: a slurred phrase is played on one breath, and a loud slurred phrase needs fewer notes per breath): Hn 3-4 breathe after 242 before the f entry at 243, Hn 4 again after 246; Hn 4 held 235-250 (about 27 s, p to f, 243-246 under one slur) without a breath mark. No note changed.
sHeadHnFour = { e1\p | f1 | f1 | g1 | c'1\mf | a1 | a1 | a1 \breathe | gis2\f( g2~ | g1 | f1~ | f1) \breathe | }
% c046 (playability; Adler PDF 292, 309, 321: a slurred phrase is one breath, brass need room to breathe): 246 Tpt 1-2 and Hn 1 end the horn call on beat 3 (were whole notes into 247, where they re-enter ff with no gap after holding C6)
% c048 (expression; A&S Unit 3, PDF 53-54: a four-bar hypermeasure leads to its goal on the next strong bar): 244-246 Tpt 1-2 and Hn 1-2 crescendo with the strings and wood-winds into ff at 247 (they held f under the others' crescendo)
% c084 (Casanelles PDF 277, 289; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 41: brass grow brilliant in the high register): Tpt 1-2 no crescendo 244-246 and f (not ff) at 247-252, where their S head (E5-C6) lies in the P head's register (Fl 1-2, Vln I-II G5-A6); Hn 1-2 stay ff; the trumpets' ff is kept for the last chorale (255)
sHeadTptOne = { R1*8 | e''2->(\f c'''2~ | c'''4 b''2.~ | b''2 c'''2~ | c'''2.) r4 | }
sHeadTptTwo = { R1*8 | e'2->(\f c''2~ | c''4 b'2.~ | b'2 c''2~ | c''2.) r4 | }
% c076 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. I, PDF 41: horns are half as strong as trumpets in forte, so mark them one degree stronger): Hn 1-2 ff on the horn call, Tpt 1-2 f
sHeadHnOne = { R1*8 | e'2->(\ff c''2~ | c''4 b'2.~ | b'2 c''2~ | c''2.) r4 | }
sHeadHnTwo = { R1*8 | e2->(\ff c'2~ | c'4 b2.~ | b2 c'2~ | c'2.) g4 | }
% c045 (Adler part 2 PDF 80, Ex. XII-48: a longer roll is written as tied notes; an untied roll restarts at each barline): rolls on one pitch tied across the barlines
% c236 (playability; Adler ch. 12, PDF 80: "a beautiful pianissimo can be achieved by playing even closer to the rim"; the usual spot is about six inches in): the pp C roll 235-238 under the
% S head, harp and sul A violins is played near the rim (the scherzo's timpani end ppp near the rim); "ord." at 243 for the p < f roll. Notes and dynamics unchanged.
sHeadTimp = { c1:32\pp~_\markup \small \italic "near the rim" | c1:32~ | c1:32~ | c1:32 | R1*4 | r2 g,2:32\p\<~_\markup \small \italic "ord." | g,1:32~ | g,1:32~ | g,1:32\f | }
% c116 (playability; Adler ch. 4, PDF 111 footnote: the two lowest strings, C1 and D1, have no pedal and are tuned by hand; PDF 114: tenths and octaves lie easily, the middle register is rich; PDF 117: damping): 239-242 harp f with the tutti (was mf under f strings, woodwinds and bassoons; its eighths are the passage's only continuous motion), LH in octaves A1-A2, F1-F2, D2-D3, D2-D3 (242 was D1 alone, the unpedalled string); 243 étouffez (printed once, between the staves) for both hands, so the D7 arpeggio and the low D do not ring into the E-major chord (G sharp, B).
sHeadHarpR = { g8 c'8 e'8 g'8 g'8 e'8 c'8 g8 | a8 c'8 f'8 a'8 a'8 f'8 c'8 a8 | a8 d'8 f'8 a'8 a'8 f'8 d'8 a8 | g8 c'8 d'8 g'8 g'8 d'8 b8 g8 | a8 c'8 e'8 a'8 a'8 e'8 c'8 a8 | a8 c'8 f'8 a'8 a'8 f'8 c'8 a8 | a8 d'8 f'8 a'8 a'8 f'8 d'8 a8 | a8\> c'8 f'8 a'8 a'8 f'8 c'8 a8 | R1*4\! | }
% c082 (A&S Unit 27, PDF 469; Unit 21 sec. 12): 238 harp's first half C4 (was B3), so the tune's C6 appoggiatura over V7 is not sounded with its tone of resolution B; B returns on beats 3-4 with the tune.
% c075 (MOLA PDF 5): pedal diagram score-only (tag 'score)
sHeadHarpL = { c,1\p-\tag #'score ^\markup \small \harp-pedal #"---|----" | c,1 | d,1 | g,1 | \ottava #-1 \set Staff.ottavation = #"8" <a,, a,>1\mf | <f,, f,>1 | <d, d>1 | <d, d>1 \ottava #0 | r1^\markup \italic "étouffez" | R1*3 | }
